{"name":"Prussian Nights","hex":"#0b085f","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"nights","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["At night (during night-time, especially on a regular basis)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["plural of night"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nights"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prussian Nights","blocks":[{"text":"Prussian Nights is a long poem by Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn, who served as a captain in the Soviet Red Army during the Second World War. Prussian Nights describes the Red Army's march across East Prussia, and focuses on the traumatic acts of rape and murder that Solzhenitsyn witnessed as a participant in that march."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9a/Solzh_Prussian_Nights.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prussian_Nights"},{"title":"Prussia","blocks":[{"text":"Prussia was a German state centred on the North European Plain. It originated from the 1525 secularization act of the Prussian part of the State of the Teutonic Order. For centuries, the House of Hohenzollern ruled Prussia, expanding its size with the Prussian Army. Prussia, with its capital at Königsberg and then, when it became the Kingdom of Prussia in 1701, Berlin, decisively shaped the history of Germany. Prussia formed the German Empire when it united the German states in 1871. It was de facto dissolved by an emergency decree transferring powers of the Prussian government to German Chancellor Franz von Papen in 1932 and de jure by an Allied decree in 1947."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281803-1892%29.svg/330px-Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Prussia_%281803-1892%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prussia"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Ordensstaat-kirchlich.jpg/500px-Ordensstaat-kirchlich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/22/Ordensstaat-kirchlich.jpg/500px-Ordensstaat-kirchlich.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Situation after the conquest in the late 13th century. Areas in purple under control of the Monastic State of the Teutonic Knights.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrdensstaat-kirchlich.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Teutonic_state_1466.png/500px-Teutonic_state_1466.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Teutonic_state_1466.png/500px-Teutonic_state_1466.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Teutonic Order (orange) following the Second Peace of Thorn (1466)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATeutonic_state_1466.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Prussian_Homage.jpg/500px-Prussian_Homage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Prussian_Homage.jpg/500px-Prussian_Homage.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Prussian Homage by Jan Matejko. After admitting the dependence of Prussia to the Polish Crown, Albert of Prussia receives Ducal Prussia as a fief from King Sigismund I the Old of Poland in 1525.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APrussian_Homage.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Nason%2C_Pieter_-_Kurf%C3%BCrst_Friedrich_Wilhelm_und_seine_Gemahlin_Luise_Henriette_-_1666_-_SPSG_2.jpg/500px-Nason%2C_Pieter_-_Kurf%C3%BCrst_Friedrich_Wilhelm_und_seine_Gemahlin_Luise_Henriette_-_1666_-_SPSG_2.jpg?ut